    visa_content_1: "<p>Common family-based pathways we help clients pursue:</p><ul><li><strong>IR-1/CR-1 (Spouse of a U.S. Citizen):</strong> for spouses of U.S. citizens; CR-1 applies to marriages under two years and converts to IR-1 status after the second anniversary.</li><li><strong>IR-2:</strong> unmarried child under 21 of a U.S. citizen.</li><li><strong>IR-5:</strong> parent of a U.S. citizen (petitioner must be 21+).</li><li><strong>F1:</strong> unmarried adult children of U.S. citizens.</li><li><strong>F2A:</strong> spouses and minor children of green card holders.</li><li><strong>F2B:</strong> unmarried adult children of green card holders.</li><li><strong>F3:</strong> married children of U.S. citizens.</li><li><strong>F4:</strong> siblings of U.S. citizens (petitioner must be 21+).</li><li><strong>K-1 (Fiancé[e] Visa):</strong> for engaged partners of U.S. citizens planning to marry within 90 days of entry.</li></ul><p><strong>General requirements:</strong> proof of the qualifying relationship, a financial sponsor who meets income guidelines (Affidavit of Support), and passing standard admissibility screening.</p><p class=\"fine-print\">Family preference categories (F1–F4) are subject to annual numerical limits, which often means a multi-year wait depending on the applicant's country of origin.</p>",
    visa_content_2: "<p>Common employment-based pathways:</p><p><strong>Temporary work visas:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>H-1B (Specialty Occupation):</strong> requires a bachelor's degree or equivalent in a specific field and a sponsoring employer; subject to an annual lottery.</li><li><strong>L-1A/L-1B (Intracompany Transferee):</strong> for managers, executives, or specialized-knowledge employees transferring from an overseas affiliate.</li><li><strong>O-1 (Extraordinary Ability):</strong> for individuals with sustained national or international acclaim in their field.</li><li><strong>TN (USMCA Professionals):</strong> for eligible Canadian and Mexican professionals in designated occupations.</li></ul><p><strong>Employment-based green cards:</strong></p><ul><li><strong>EB-1:</strong> extraordinary ability, outstanding researchers/professors, or multinational executives/managers (often no labor certification required).</li><li><strong>EB-2:</strong> advanced degree professionals or those with exceptional ability; may qualify for a National Interest Waiver without a job offer.</li><li><strong>EB-3:</strong> skilled workers, professionals, and other workers; typically requires a PERM labor certification.</li><li><strong>EB-4:</strong> certain special immigrants (e.g., religious workers).</li><li><strong>EB-5:</strong> investors who make a qualifying investment in a U.S. business that creates jobs.</li></ul><p class=\"fine-print\">Most categories require either a sponsoring U.S. employer and, often, a certified labor market test (PERM), or evidence of extraordinary/exceptional qualifications.</p>",
    visa_content_3: "<p>General naturalization eligibility (Form N-400):</p><ul><li>Lawful permanent resident status for at least 5 years (or 3 years if married to and living with a U.S. citizen).</li><li>Continuous residence and physical presence in the U.S. for the required periods.</li><li>Good moral character during the statutory period.</li><li>Basic English proficiency and knowledge of U.S. civics (some applicants qualify for age- or disability-based exemptions).</li><li>Attachment to the principles of the U.S. Constitution.</li></ul><p class=\"fine-print\">Eligibility rules have exceptions for military service members and certain other applicants.</p>",
    visa_content_4: "<p>Basic E-2 requirements:</p><ul><li>The investor must be a national of a country that has a qualifying treaty of commerce with the United States.</li><li>A substantial, at-risk investment in a real and operating U.S. business.</li><li>The investor must develop and direct the enterprise, typically through at least 50% ownership or operational control.</li><li>The business must be more than marginal — it must generate more than enough income to support the investor and family, or have significant economic impact.</li></ul><p class=\"fine-print\">The E-2 is a renewable nonimmigrant visa; it does not by itself lead to a green card.</p>",
    visa_content_5: "<p>Common forms of humanitarian relief:</p><ul><li><strong>Asylum:</strong> for individuals in the U.S. who fear persecution in their home country based on race, religion, nationality, political opinion, or membership in a particular social group; generally must be filed within one year of arrival, with some exceptions.</li><li><strong>VAWA Self-Petition:</strong> allows an abused spouse, child, or parent of a U.S. citizen or green card holder to self-petition for immigration status without the abuser's knowledge or participation.</li><li><strong>U Visa:</strong> for victims of certain qualifying crimes who have suffered substantial harm and are helpful to law enforcement in the investigation or prosecution.</li></ul><p class=\"fine-print\">These are highly fact-specific cases; timing and evidence requirements vary significantly by category.</p>",
    visa_content_6: "<p>Two paths to a green card once a petition is approved:</p><ul><li><strong>Consular Processing:</strong> used when the applicant is outside the United States; the immigrant visa is issued at a U.S. embassy or consulate abroad.</li><li><strong>Adjustment of Status (Form I-485):</strong> used when the applicant is already in the United States in a qualifying status; results in a green card without having to leave the country, and typically includes a biometrics appointment and interview.</li></ul><p class=\"fine-print\">Which path applies depends on where the applicant is located and their current immigration status.</p>",
    visa_content_7: "<p>Common matters we assist with:</p><ul><li><strong>Removal Defense:</strong> representation in immigration court (EOIR) proceedings, including applications for relief such as cancellation of removal, asylum, or withholding of removal.</li><li><strong>I-601 Waiver:</strong> waives certain grounds of inadmissibility (e.g., specific misrepresentation or criminal grounds).</li><li><strong>I-601A Provisional Waiver:</strong> allows certain applicants to request a waiver of unlawful presence before leaving the U.S. for consular processing.</li><li><strong>I-212:</strong> permission to reapply for admission after a prior removal or deportation.</li></ul><p class=\"fine-print\">Waiver eligibility usually requires showing extreme hardship to a qualifying U.S. citizen or green card holder relative.</p>",
    visa_content_8: "<p>Overview of these statuses:</p><ul><li><strong>DACA (Deferred Action for Childhood Arrivals):</strong> for individuals who came to the U.S. as children and meet continuous residence, age, and education/military-service requirements; provides deferred action from removal and work authorization, renewable every two years (does not itself lead to a green card).</li><li><strong>TPS (Temporary Protected Status):</strong> available to eligible nationals of countries designated by the U.S. government due to armed conflict, natural disaster, or other extraordinary conditions; provides temporary protection from removal and work authorization during the designation period.</li></ul><p class=\"fine-print\">Both DACA and TPS require timely registration/renewal and are subject to change based on government policy.</p>",
    visa_modal_disclaimer: "This is general information only and not legal advice. Your eligibility depends on your specific circumstances — schedule a consultation to discuss your case.",

